This paper provides a critical review of the state-of-the-art understanding of MP transport in soil. We point to several non-intuitive and often contradictory findings, demonstrating how these can be explained by the interference among factors and processes. Finally, we outline the knowledge gaps and propose future research directions.
2. Methodology and paper structure
To identify relevant papers, we conducted a literature search with the search term “microplastic” AND (“transport” OR “move” OR “mobile” OR “mobility”) AND (“soil” OR “porous media” OR “sand”) – applied to title, abstract, keywords and full publication text. The search was conducted using the Google Scholar, PubMed, and Web of Science databases, and focused on papers published since 2000 (search last updated 22 July 2024; see Table S1 in SI for search details). Results were sorted according to relevance, and the searched literature was manually screened for papers that explicitly investigated the factors that affect microplastic transport in soil, excluding papers that coincidentally met the search criteria while being nonrelevant (e.g. papers that focused on NPs but mention “microplastics” in the text). Similarly, papers that focused on MP transport in aquatic environments (while mentioning briefly soils) or on the effect of MPs on transport of other substances were also excluded. Overall, we retained 113 papers that specifically addressed MP migration in soil, including 50 experimental studies. We note that our search results include papers that define particles in the 0.1 μm – 1.0 μm range as “microplastics” (as per the definition of Koelmans et al., 2015), while excluding papers that define such particles as “nanoplastics” (as per the definition of Weber et al., 2022).
Many of the studies on MP transport in soil were conducted using artificially constructed soil analogs such as quartz sand or glass beads. These experiments, by simplifying visualization and/or analysis, provide invaluable insights into the transport of MPs in natural soils and thus are included in this review and analysis. However, contributions on controlled systems with even more idealized media (such as microfluidic channels), aimed to enhance fundamental understanding of specific aspects (e.g., MP entrainment in liquid films; Wu et al., 2021), did not fall within our search criteria and hence not included in this review. In the rest of the paper, when making general statements for which the distinction between natural soils and artificial soil analogs is not crucial, we use the terms soil and porous matrix interchangeably. We also stress that in some experiments, the settings used could be different than in typical natural conditions, for instance in terms of how the water and MPs were introduced, or the MPs concentration. Our review includes these papers since some of the parameters in the natural environment, for instance, rainfall intensity, soil solution geochemistry, soil pore and particle sizes, MPs sizes, and concentration can fall within a huge range of values, making the insights from these studies invaluable. Even for parameters for which there is a relatively narrow range which considered typical, with time these ranges could be substantially extended; as an example, while the initial MP concentration is typically considered to be of only a few %, their transport in heterogenous soils containing preferential pathways could create “MP hotspots” with much higher MP concentrations (Cramer et al., 2023). The paper is structured such that each section focuses on the impact of a group of properties on MP transport while pointing to their coupling with others. Sections 3–5 overview the impact of MP properties, soil properties, and the hydrological conditions, respectively. Section 6 overviews the effect of living organisms. Section 7 provides concluding remarks, summarizing the research gaps and suggesting future directions. Additionally, Tables S2 and S3 in the SI list the experimental conditions classified in terms of the MP and soil properties, respectively.
3. Microplastic properties
This section focuses on the following MP properties: size, type, and shape, and their derivatives such as density, hydrophobicity, surface roughness, hydrodynamic diameter, and Zeta potential. We also discuss here MP aging, as a process altering other MP properties.
3.1. Microplastic particle size
A basic property that has a substantial influence on MP mobility in soil is MP size. Since MP size affects multiple mechanisms which control MP mobility, there is a complex trend: in general, MP mobility in soil for larger MPs (≳1 μm) is inversely proportional to the MP size, and it was found in terms of different MP mobility metrics (Table 1), including maximum depth (Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022b; O'Connor et al., 2019; Tumwet et al., 2022), proportion of MPs reaching the maximum depth (Cohen and Radian, 2022), mass recovery rate (Rong et al., 2022), and normalized concentration (Wang et al., 2022c). This is rather intuitive: for a given size of soil pores, smaller MPs can traverse further (Gao et al., 2021; Ranjan et al., 2023; O'Connor et al., 2019). In contrast, Qi et al. (2022) found that 20 μm MPs had higher mobility than 10 μm MPs, i.e. when water flow rate was 4 mL/min, the MP concentrations of 10 and 20 μm MPs, went through soil, were 0.85 and 1.49 items/mL, respectively. The authors explained that smaller MP particles have higher affinity to soil particles, which reduces MP mobility. For 0.1 μm ≲ MP ≲1 μm, increasing MP size increases MP mobility (in terms of transport rate in Gui et al. (2022)). The reason that the trend in MP size vs. MP mobility reverses for very small MPs is linked to the fact that for very small particles, other forces and mechanisms become dominant. In particular, the Zeta potential, a measure of the electrical charge at the surface of particles suspended in solution (Bhattacharjee, 2016), plays an important role. For example, in several studies, for MPs ≲1 μm, the Zeta potential of MP becomes less negative with decreasing MP size (Fig. 1; Wang et al., 2022e; Gui et al., 2022), and, therefore, decreasing the repulsive force between MPs and soil particles, enhancing their retention in soil (Gui et al., 2022). However, the experiments from Cai et al. (2019) show a different result whereby the Zeta potential of 0.2 μm MP is more negative than that of 1 μm (Fig. 1). The relationship between MP size and MP Zeta potential is also related to environmental factors such as pH and ionic strength of soil solution (Fig. 1; Table S4 in SI).
