At even smaller plastic particle sizes (<0.1 μm), different mechanisms can become dominant, thus reversing the trend again. For plastic particles smaller than 0.1 μm, Wang et al., 2022e and Li et al. (2019) showed that mobility increased with decreasing particle size (smaller particles exhibited higher total mass recovery and mass recovery rate). The authors attributed this to hydrodynamic diameter, which is the diameter of the particles including their electronic layers (Maguire et al., 2018), implying this mechanism can become dominant for very small particles. The dependence of MP mobility with MP size is intimately linked with the MP concentration and soil pore sizes; see discussion in Sections 3.4 and 4.1. As a result of the aforementioned interdependencies among mechanisms, establishing the critical size at which the MP mobility trend reverses is not straightforward, as it can depend on other various properties. This can explain the apparent discrepancy between a critical size of 2 μm (for which MP normalized concentrations were higher than for 0.4 μm and 10 μm; Wang et al., 2022c) and 1 μm (for which MP mass recovery was greater than for 0.2 μm and 2 μm; Rong et al., 2022).
3.2. Microplastic shape
Microplastics are found in natural soils in a variety of shapes (Fig. S2 in SI), with the most common being fragments (irregular-shaped), fibers, films, foams, and beads (spheres) (Sajjad et al., 2022). While it is accepted that MP shapes affect their transport, the link between MP shapes and their transport in soils remains elusive, also due to the large number of additional parameters which vary among experiments probing the impact of MP shapes (Tables S2-S3 in SI). Beads were found to reach deeper than films because films were trapped more easily by plant roots (Li et al., 2021a). Fragments were found to move deeper than fibers (Gao et al., 2021; Cohen and Radian, 2022; Waldschläger and Schüttrumpf, 2020), possibly due to the tendency of fibers to become entangled with soil grains (de Souza Machado et al., 2018; Waldschläger and Schüttrumpf, 2020; Keller et al., 2019). Similarly, spheres reach a deeper layer than fragments (irregular-shaped) because fragments can easily be entangled with soil particles (Waldschläger and Schüttrumpf, 2020). Zhang et al. (2022b), fibers reached deeper than fragments, which in turn were more mobile than foams and films. However, it is hard to draw conclusive evidence regarding MP fiber mobility from Zhang et al. (2022b), as the authors did not specify the fiber diameter, which has a greater effect on MP infiltration than its length (Waldschläger and Schüttrumpf, 2020). The above suggests that fiber, fragments, and beads tend to migrate deeper than films and foams.
3.3. Microplastics type
Different MP types (made from different polymers) have different properties including density, hydrophobicity (contact angle), surface roughness, and Zeta potential (O'Connor et al., 2019; Fei et al., 2022; Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Cohen and Radian, 2022). Each of these properties can affect various aspects of MP transport. In general, heavier MPs have greater vertical mobility. For example, polyethylene (PE; density of 0.884–0.898 g/cm3) reached deeper than polypropylene (PP; density of 0.833 g/cm3) (O'Connor et al., 2019). However, surface properties often dominate over density: lower hydrophobicity, higher Zeta potential, and lower roughness were found to have a stronger positive effect on MP transport in soil than higher density (Fei et al., 2022; Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Cohen and Radian, 2022). Despite its lower density, the mass recovery rate of polylactic acid (PLA; density of 1.25 g/cm3) was higher than polyvinyl chloride (PVC; density of 1.38 g/cm3), because PLA has higher Zeta potential and lower hydrophobicity (Fei et al., 2022). Comparing PE, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and PP, the order of PE > PET>PP was identified in terms of depth reached (Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021), again explained by the lower hydrophobicity and higher Zeta potential of PE vs. PET vs. PP, despite its lower density (all other conditions were uniform). The lower mobility of PP was explained by its low density and Zeta potential, and its higher hydrophobicity and surface roughness (Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021). Lower hydrophobicity was used to explain deeper penetration of polyamide (PA) (vs. PE, PET, and PP in Gao et al., 2021; and vs. PET in terms of depth ratio in Cohen and Radian, 2022). Inconsistencies between studies, e.g. deeper penetration of PET vs. PE in Gao et al. (2021), opposite to the trend found by Zhang et al. (2022b), could be due to the different mobility metrics: PET exhibited larger penetration depth than PE, however its relative mobility was lower (Zhang et al. (2022b)). Additional potential sources of inconsistency are due to using different analytical methods for sample preparation and analysis of MPs in the MP transport experiments (Table S5). Differences in other experimental parameters, including the soil properties, hydraulic conditions, and MP size and shape might also cause inconsistency between studies.
3.4. Microplastic concentration
The concentration of MPs does not always correlate with their mobility. Xu et al., 2022a, Xu et al., 2022b showed that higher concentration of MPs (for two sizes, 0.1 μm and 2 μm) resulted in a higher mass recovery rate. This is due to the adsorption of some MPs on soil particles which then depleted the available sites for adsorption with increasing MP concentration and, therefore, enhancing MP mobility. Contrasting findings in smaller particles (≤ 0.1 μm), where the mobility (total recovery rate) showed inverse correlation with their concentration (Wang et al., 2022e). Contradictory findings for plastic particles ≤0.1 μm with increasing their concentration are probably due to the increasing dominance of mechanisms as plastic particle size decreases, including: (i) aggregation and deposition (Wang et al., 2022e); (ii) adsorption on soil particles due to the increase in plastic particle specific surface area (Shen et al., 2019); as well as (iii) clogging of pores by plastic particles. This contraction may also be due to differences in other MPs and soil properties between these studies (Tables S2-S3 in SI). Flow rates and soil particle size can also interplay with MP concentrations to affect MP transport. For example, in 1–2 mm sand, while at high-enough water flow rates (2.5 mL/min in Hou et al., 2020) MP mobility shows the expected proportionality with MP concentration, at lower flow rates (1–2 mL/min) no such correlation was found (Hou et al., 2020). A possible explanation is that MPs can be more easily intercepted by sand particles at slow flow rates, with faster flow rates allowing easier flushing of MPs reducing such interceptions (Hou et al., 2020). Additionally, at a water flow rate of 1–1.5 mL/min, the MP normalized concentration increased with increase of MP concentration in 2–4 mm sand, while at 1–2 mm sand no such correlation was found. A possible explanation is that the blockage of MP particles in finer soils is more apparent (Hou et al., 2020).
3.5. Microplastic aging
Aging of MPs, which can occur by oxidation, hydrolysis, biodegradation, photodegradation, and mechanical processes, can alter a variety of physical and chemical properties of MPs. The mobility of aged MPs was found to be greater than that of pristine MPs, in terms of both MP concentration (Qi et al., 2022) and maximum depth (Wang et al., 2022b). One reason is that aging increases MP oxygen-containing groups (Ali et al., 2023), which can reduce MP adsorption on soil particles (Qi et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2020). Furthermore, aging alters polymer structure, reducing MP size and changing their shape (Ren et al., 2021; Yan et al., 2020). For example, Mao et al. (2020) found that the CH bond of microplastics breaks under the action of UV light, causing MP to break into smaller particles (Mao et al., 2020). Breaking into smaller MPs, which is known to enhance MP mobility (Ranjan et al., 2023), as well as reduce MP hydrodynamic diameter (Wang et al., 2022b). Aging also increases MP Zeta potential, and hence the interaction energy between MPs and soil particles (Wang et al., 2022b), and can increase MP surface hydrophilicity, promoting MP transport in soil (Gunther et al., 2023; Li et al., 2021a). Notably, aging can increase the surface roughness of MP (Luo et al., 2023a), which may lead to interlocking between the rough surfaces of MPs and the irregularly shaped soil particles, thereby decreasing MP mobility. Further research is needed to evaluate the relative impact of this mechanism vs. that of other, competing effects of MP aging (which facilitate MP migration) including reduced size, increased surface functionality, and enhanced hydrophilicity.
4. Soil physicochemical properties
This section reviews the impact of soil physical and chemical properties including soil texture, grain/pore size, soil pH, ionic strength (IS), soil mineralogy, and organic components, on MP transport. We also include here the impact of chemical substances present in the soil, as, while they are not soil properties per se, they do affect soil chemistry.
4.1. Soil texture, grain and pore size
Overall, coarser soil texture promotes MP transport; this was found in terms of mass recovery rate (Dong et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2020), maximum depth (Mancini et al., 2023), transport rate (Gui et al., 2022), MP concentration (number of MPs per kg soil) at maximum depth (Xing et al., 2021), and loss ratio (Rehm et al., 2021). For a given MP size, larger soil particles, which in turn imply larger pore sizes, enhance MP mobility in soil, in terms of mass recovery rate (Dong et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2020), maximum depth (Gao et al., 2021; Ranjan et al., 2023), and MP concentration (Qi et al., 2022). The control parameter when varying both MP size and soil texture can be evaluated using the ratio of the diameter of MP particles (DMP) vs. that of the soil particles (DS), where a smaller ratio promotes MP transport in soil (Waldschläger and Schüttrumpf, 2020). The critical ratio can be evaluated theoretically by considering the size of a constriction in the simple case of a hexagonal packing of equal-sized spheres: the narrowest aperture (between each triplet of particles) is ∼0.1DS, indicating a critical ratio of ∼0.1. Experimental data agrees with this estimation: MPs were found to be highly mobile for DMP/DS < 0.1, and nearly immobile for DMP/DS > 0.3 (Gao et al., 2021; Ranjan et al., 2023). Similarly, the infiltration depth of MPs was found to reduce from 13.1 cm to 4.63 cm, and 1.18 cm at DMP/DS < 0.11, 0.11 < DMP/DS < 0.32, and DMP/DS > 0.32, respectively. However, we note that because most soils are composed of a wide range of particle sizes, the average grain diameter DS is insufficient to describe the impact of soil texture on MP mobility, which may require to consider a more comprehensive parameter such as particle size distribution. In addition to the obvious effect of larger soil pores in coarse-textured soils (Dong et al., 2022; Xing et al., 2021), larger soil particles have lower specific surface area, and therefore, adsorb and retain MPs less than finer soils. Soil texture also affects soil Zeta potential. In most cases, both soil and MP surfaces are negatively charged; in such cases, coarser soils have higher Zeta potential (Dong et al., 2022; Rong et al., 2022; Gui et al., 2022; Li et al., 2023), and thus stronger repulsion promoting MP mobility (in terms of mass recovery rate in Wu et al., 2020). MP surface roughness was found to reduce MP penetration depth, due to increased friction with the soil pores (Ranjan et al., 2023). However, this effect is subject to interference with other mechanisms and properties, including water flow rate and MP concentration (Hou et al., 2020). Higher porosity was also found to promote MP mass recovery rate (Li et al., 2023; Dong et al., 2022). We stress however that porosity, being a scalar which represents a sample-averaged property, is a too simplistic soil characteristic to provide a clear trend of MP mobility. For instance, soil can have high porosity which is poorly connected (e.g. with dead-end pores), such that it has limited ability to transport water and MPs. This could potentially explain the seemingly-contradictory result of higher MP mass recovery rate in low-porosity desert soil than in high-porosity red soil (Wu et al., 2020).
4.2. Mineralogy of soil grains
Soil mineralogy affects MP transport in two ways. Firstly, forming MP-minerals aggregates, which have a higher density than MPs alone, can enhance the depth of MPs penetration (Yan et al., 2020). Secondly, MP-minerals aggregates can decrease MP mobility in soil. For instance, Li et al. (2019), Li et al. (2020a), and Gui et al. (2022) found that the forming of MP-Fe/Al oxide aggregates decreased the MP mass recovery rate and transport rate because the diameter of the MP-Fe/Al oxide aggregates was larger than individual MP particles. The effect of MP-minerals aggregates on MP transport might be MP size dependent (< 5 mm in Yan et al., 2020, vs. 0.02–2 μm in Li et al., 2019; Li et al., 2020a, vs. 0.3–1 μm in Gui et al., 2022). Koutnik et al. (2022a) found that the MP density plays a greater role for the movement of larger MPs (> 50 μm); however, the intermolecular force plays a greater role than MP density for the movement of small MPs (<50 μm).
Soil minerals affect MP transport by affecting the MP Zeta potential and the interaction of MPs and minerals (Yan et al., 2020; Li et al., 2019; Li et al., 2020a; Li et al., 2021b; Wu et al., 2020; Gui et al., 2022). Yan et al. (2020) found that natural soil minerals (soil minerals were not specified by authors) enhanced the penetration depth of MPs because they make the MP Zeta potential more negative. In contrast, Wu et al. (2020) and Gui et al. (2022) found that the presence of Fe or Al oxide minerals (Fe/Al oxides) decreased MP mobility (both mass recovery rate and transport rate) because MPs were adsorbed on the surface of Fe/Al oxides. Fe/Al oxides were also found to limit MP transport by reducing the volume of larger pores (Lu et al., 2014). Thus, highly weathered soils such as Ultisols, which typically contain significant quantities of Fe and Al oxides, may act as barriers to MP movement. The contrasting findings of Yan et al. (2020) versus Wu et al. (2020) and Gui et al. (2022) might be due to difference in the electrical properties of different minerals, as well as the different mobility metrics (rate- vs. depth-based). The effect of Fe and Al oxides on MP transport is pH-dependent because their surface charge is altered depends on pH: it is positive for acidic and neutral pH, and it becomes negative above pH 8 (for example in hematite, due to the deprotonation of Fe-OH; Nie et al., 2023). Kaolinite and bentonite particles were found to increase the mass recovery rate of MPs at pH 6 and IS 5 mM NaCl because they can act as a carrier for MPs, thus promoting MP mobility (Li et al., 2020a; Li et al., 2021b). However, bentonite would decrease the MP mobility at IS of 25 mM NaCl because the diameter of MP-bentonite aggregates was around 3 times larger than individual MP particle (Li et al., 2021b).
4.3. Organic components in soils
Soil organic matter enhances MP transport (Ivanic et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Dong et al., 2021; Hou et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022e; Xu et al., 2022a, Xu et al., 2022b; Zhao et al., 2022a; Xu et al., 2024b). For instance, dissolved organic matter was found to increase MP normalized concentration by increasing the MP surface wettability and increasing the dispersion of MP particles on the soil pore surfaces (Ivanic et al., 2023; Xu et al., 2024b). Humic acid (HA) was found to enhance the maximal MP penetration depth by increasing repulsion between the MPs and soil particles and possibly decreasing MP hydrophobicity (Fig. 2b; Gao et al., 2021). Further studies showed that HA decreased the roughness of the MP surface, promoting the MP mass recovery rate (Zhao et al., 2022a; Dong et al., 2021, Dong et al., 2022). Similarly, fulvic acid (FA) was found to increase MP normalized concentration by increasing Zeta potential of both MPs and sand particles and thus their repulsive force (represented in terms of maximum repulsive energy controlling adsorption; Wang et al., 2022e; Fig. 2a∼b). However, Hou et al. (2020) demonstrated that the MP mass recovery rate was not always increased with increasing FA concentration because for high FA concentration (> 5 mg/L) other factors such as hydrophobicity and buoyancy can promote MP transport over electrostatic forces (Hou et al., 2020). HA has greater transport-promoting for MPs than FA because HA has a larger molecular weight and more complex structure, leading to a stronger repulsive force to MP particles than that of FA (Liu et al., 2024). Soil colloids were shown to promote MP mass recovery rate by filling the concave area of the soil pores (in the contact between the grains), reducing the soil surface roughness, and increasing the repulsive force between MPs and pore walls (Fig. 3a; Xu et al., 2022a, Xu et al., 2022b). Soils rich in organic matter are conducive to MP transport: beyond the direct interaction between MP and organic matter, the organic matter also improves soil structure by forming secondary aggregates and enhancing soil biological activity (Tarafdar, 2022), creating stable porous pathways for MP transport. Notably, commonly used agricultural organic supplements (e.g. manure and sewage sludge) also contain MPs (Zhang et al., 2022c).
