4.4. pH
Both MP mass recovery rate and normalized concentration were found to increase with increasing pH (Fei et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022e). This is due to the Zeta potential of both MPs and soil minerals becoming more negative with increasing pH (Fig. 3b∼d), indicating a stronger repulsive force between them (Fig. 2a∼b). The repulsion (associated with electrostatic forces) was suggested as the dominant factor promoting MP mass recovery rate with increasing pH (Wu et al., 2020). Fei et al. (2022) and Wang et al. (2022e) found that the MP hydrodynamic diameter increases with decreasing pH and, therefore, decreasing MP mass recovery. Nie et al. (2023) found that increasing pH from 3 to 8.5 reversed the interaction force of MPs and minerals (from attraction to repulsion), decreasing MP-minerals aggregation (Nie et al., 2023). Overall, the repulsive force between MP and sand as well as other soil minerals increases with increasing pH (Fig. 2b).
4.5. Ionic strength
In general, MP mobility decreases with increasing IS (Chu et al., 2019; Dong et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022e; Li et al., 2021b; found in terms of both MP mass recovery rate, and MP normalized concentration). The Zeta potential of both soil particles and MPs increases with increasing IS (Wang et al., 2022e; Hou et al., 2020; Cao et al., 2023). The repulsive force between sand and microplastic increased with decreasing IS (Chu et al., 2019). Moreover, the hydrodynamic diameter of MPs increases with increasing IS, which leads to enhanced retention and reduced transport of MPs (Cao et al., 2023). Different types of MPs can show relatively different mobility at different IS due to specific characteristics (e.g., chemical composition; presence of functional groups on the surface of MPs). For example, Cao et al. (2023) found that the total mass recovery of PVC and PP MPs was relatively lower than PMMA and PET MPs for increasing IS. It was found that Ca2+ has a greater effect on MP retention than K+, Na+, and Mg2+ (Dong et al., 2021), and that Al3+ has a greater effect on MP retention than Na+ and Mg2+ (Fei et al., 2022) because cations with higher ionic radii lead to stronger charge screening, which increases particle retention (Dong et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2020). In soil contaminated by MPs, different IS have different effects on the MP migration because the degree of aggregation of soil mineral particles with MPs can be different under different IS. For example, ∼77 % and ∼ 97 % of bentonites were adsorbed onto MPs forming MP-bentonite hetero-aggregates when IS was 5 mM and 25 mM NaCl, respectively; the diameter of the MP-bentonite hetero-aggregates increased with increasing IS, which in turn decreased the MP mass recovery rate (Li et al., 2021b).
4.6. Chemical substances in soils
Chemical substances that might be present in soil, such as nitrogen fertilizers, antibiotics, nonylphenol, emerging pollutants (e.g., perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA), as part of per- and polyfluorinated substances (PFAS)), and biochar, can also impact MP transport. Nitrogen fertilizers were found to decrease the MP mass recovery rate because they decreased the Zeta potential of both MPs and soil particles and increased the hydrodynamic diameter of MPs (Fig. 2a, c; Rong et al., 2022). Chlortetracycline hydrochloride (a widely used antibiotic in agriculture) was shown to decrease the MP mass recovery rate, due to a decline in the electrostatic repulsion between MP and sand grains (Xu et al., 2022b). The large specific surface area of biochar was shown to provide more adsorption sites for MPs, hindering their transport (Wang et al., 2022d), with the stronger impact of Fe3O4 biochar because its surface roughness, specific surface area, and the total pore volume are much higher than normal biochar, indicating that the Fe3O4 biochar is more beneficial to the MP retention (Wang et al., 2022f; Tong et al., 2020). Unlike biochar and nitrogen fertilizers, nonylphenol (a non-ionic surfactant present in pesticide formulation) increases the MP mass recovery rate, as it increases the MP Zeta potential, and possibly because of the hydrogen bonds forming between MPs and nonylphenol (Xu et al., 2022a).
The presence of surfactants in soil alters the surface hydrophobicity of microplastic, influencing MP transport (Sun et al., 2022; Jiang et al., 2021). Both cationic surfactants and anionic surfactants were found to promote the MP mass recovery rate, as long as the surfactant concentration remained lower than the critical micelle concentration; this was explained by the increase in the Zeta potential of MPs and sand particles and the decrease of MP hydrophobicity by the surfactants (Jiang et al., 2021). Cationic surfactants were found to have a greater effect on MP transport than anionic surfactants because the former provides more osmotic and elastic repulsion forces between MPs and sand particles (Jiang et al., 2022). Surfactants have a nontrivial effect with MP types (PE and PP) on MP mobility: while the mobility of PE was higher than of PP without surfactants (in terms of maximum depth; Gao et al., 2021; O'Connor et al., 2019; Ranjan et al., 2023), the addition of surfactants reversed that trend (in terms of MP mass recovery rate; Jiang et al., 2021, Jiang et al., 2022; Zhou et al., 2024). The authors explained this by the increase in Zeta potential, as well as the larger surface area and roughness of PP MPs, promoting surfactant retention (Jiang et al., 2021). Zhou et al. (2024) attribute this to the PP hydrophilicity being higher than PE hydrophilicity when adding surfactants. However, we note that the use of two different MP mobility metrics across these studies does not allow a decisive conclusion regarding the main reason for the reversal in mobility of PP and PE. Zhao et al. (2020) found that the effect of tetracycline (TC) on the of MP mobility depends on the type of solution and IS. For example, in KCl or CaCl2 solutions at IS 1 mM, TC increased the mass recovery of MP because TC decreased MP hydrophobicity, promoting MP mobile. However, at IS 100 mM, TC decreased the MP mobility in the KCl solution but increased the MP mobility in the CaCl2 solution. The authors attributed this to the presence of TC rendered the attachment efficiencies of MP less sensitive to the changes in the CaCl2 solutions (Zhao et al., 2020). Similarly, titanium dioxide nanoparticles (nTiO2) decreased the mass recovery of MP because they formed aggregates with MPs, which increased the diameter of MP compared to individual MP (Cai et al., 2019). PFOA (a widely used industrial surfactant) was found to decrease the normalized concentration of negatively-charged MPs, thereby decreasing their Zeta potential, and promoting their adsorption on soil particles; an opposite effect—increasing normalized concentration—was observed for positively-charged MPs (carboxyl group modified MPs), due to the reversal of their surface charge, limiting their adsorption on soil particles (Rong et al., 2023). In contrast, Xu et al. (2024a) found that PFOA increases the recovery rate of MPs because PFOA induces a more negatively charged MP surface.
5. Hydrological conditions
Since transport of MPs in soils is driven by water infiltration, the hydrological conditions that control how water is introduced into the soil play a decisive role. The interaction of MPs with water is complex: in addition to the amount of water and its velocity, the transport of small MPs also depends on the motion of air-water interfaces in the vadose zone. The fact that MPs are hydrophobic further complicates their migration, as explained below.
5.1. Water flux
Higher water flux in general promotes MP transport (Fei et al., 2022; Qi et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2022c; Dong et al., 2022). This is mainly due to the increase in the shear force which sweeps MPs with the water flow; however, slower water infiltration allows more transverse (horizontal) flow, which could enhance MP transport by allowing the water to traverse larger MPs-contaminated areas in the soil (shown in terms of mass recovery rate in Hou et al. (2020) and Fei et al. (2022)). Higher water velocity was shown to increase the detachment of MPs from the soil matrix (Wang et al., 2022c; Qi et al., 2022; Dong et al., 2022). Further intricacies can arise due to the interactions of flow rates with factors including IS, MP concentration, and pore size (Dong et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2020). Dong et al. (2022) showed a positive correlation between water velocity and MP mass recovery rate under high IS (10 Mm NaCl), albeit with negligible influence of velocity on MP transport at lower IS (0.1 Mm NaCl).
Another nontrivial relationship between MP transport and water flux is linked to MP concentration and soil particle size, showing an initial increase in MP mass recovery rate with velocity followed by a decrease at even higher velocities, for MP concentrations of 0.3 mg/L and 0.5 mg/L in 1–2 and 2–4 mm sand, respectively (Hou et al., 2020). The authors attributed this decline in MP mobility to an increase in MP adsorption to soil particles due to increase in frequency of collisions between MP particles with flow rate, occupying a larger portion of the pore space. All these factors increase the probability of MPs to adsorb on the soil particles. We note most studies on the effect of flow rate were conducted in coarser soils, where flow rates would be higher than in finer soils.
5.2. Soil water saturation
Soil water saturation (the relative pore volume occupied by water, changing by wetting/drying) also affects MP transport (Dong et al., 2022). This might be due to the hydrophobic nature of MPs, minimized contact of MPs with water, and MPs can remain trapped within the air-water interface (Al Harraq and Bharti, 2022). As decrease in water saturation produces more air-water interfaces, this in turn limits MP migration in soils (Dong et al., 2022). Another reason for this trend is the increase in trapped air bubbles, that decrease the available pore space for migration of water and MPs (Dong et al., 2022). For low density MPs, however, increasing water saturation was shown to decrease MP mass recovery rate because of buoyancy effects (O'Connor et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021a).
5.3. Wetting-drying cycles
Since MP transport is strongly linked to water saturation (Dong et al., 2022) and to the air-water interfaces (Al Harraq and Bharti, 2022), cycles of wetting and drying (which cause the interfaces to advance and recede repeatedly) play a crucial role. A positive correlation between the number of cycles and the maximum MP depth was reported for various settings (Gao et al., 2021; O'Connor et al., 2019; Ranjan et al., 2023; Zhao et al., 2022b). In addition to interface motion, wetting-drying cycles are associated with soil surface damage, in particular, cracks formed by repeated shrinkage and expansion (Tang et al., 2021; Wan et al., 2019), serving as preferential pathways for MPs. Another mechanism associated with wetting-drying cycles which affects MP transport is mechanical abrasion and weathering of MPs resulting in fragmentation into smaller particles; this was used to explain increased MPs penetration depth with wetting-drying cycles (Ranjan et al., 2023).
5.4. Freeze-thaw cycles
Freeze-thaw cycles can promote MP mobility, by (1) releasing colloids from the soil to which MPs can attach and, therefore, increasing MP mobility; and (2) applying cyclic mechanical pressure on pore walls, creating additional preferential flow pathways (Li et al., 2023; Koutnik et al., 2022b). Contrasting evidence showed that these cycles caused aggregation of MPs, decreasing their mobility (in terms of MP normalized concentration in Alimi et al., 2021). The difference of plastic size was suggested as explanation for these contrasting results (Alimi et al., 2021). The impact of freeze-thaw cycles depends on the type of soil: it was found to increase mobility in loamy sand and sandy soil, with no apparent effect in quartz sand media (in terms of MP normalized concentration) (Li et al., 2023). The authors explained that the loamy sand and sandy soils released more colloids than quartz sand media. However, Koutnik et al. (2022b) found that although a sand and soil mixture (biofilter media) could release more colloids, the freeze-throw cycles increased greater MP mobility (normalized concentration) in sand than that in biofilter media because the pore sizes in sand were larger.
6. Living organisms
Living organisms such as soil animals, plants, and microorganisms, affect MP transport (Huerta Lwanga et al., 2017, Huerta Lwanga et al., 2018; Rillig et al., 2017; Li et al., 2021a; He et al., 2020, He et al., 2021), with a larger effect exhibited by larger living species (Ren et al., 2021). It has been shown that soil animals (e.g. earthworms) can promote MP migration by (1) creating macro-pores that serve as preferential MP pathways; and (2) digesting MPs, transporting them further to where MPs are either excreted as defecate or released once the organism dies (Huerta Lwanga et al., 2017; Rillig et al., 2017). Plant roots enhance the penetration depth of MP because their decomposition leaves a macropore for MP transport. MPs in contact with the root tips of plants tend to migrate with the growth of the root system; thus, MP transport will depend on the root orientation, i.e. horizontal vs. vertical (Li et al., 2021a). Rhizosphere secretion can also induce MPs aging and decrease their hydrophobicity, increasing MP mobility (Li et al., 2021a).
Microscopic organisms, i.e. bacteria, affect MP transport by altering both the soil and the MP properties (He et al., 2020, He et al., 2021). For example, He et al. (2020) found that the gram-negative strain E. coli was shown to decrease the MP mass recovery rate by creating a biofilm which narrows the soil pores as well as increases their surface roughness, as well as by decreasing the repulsive force between MPs and soil particles. Bacteria interact with MPs in a complex manner, by forming MPs-bacteria aggregates (He et al., 2021). Bacteria reduce the Zeta potential of negatively charged MPs (the Zeta potential of MPs-bacteria aggregates is lower than that of only MPs) and increase the hydrodynamic diameter (larger for aggregates than for individual MPs); both effects were shown to decrease the MP mass recovery rate. For positively charged MPs, bacteria will reverse the MP charge (as the overall charge of the aggregates is negative), increasing the MP mass recovery rate in soils with negatively charged grains. In addition, IS influences the effect trend of types of bacteria on MP transport. For example, the reductions of the mass recovery of negatively charged MP by E. coli are less than by B. subtilis (in both IS = 5 mM and IS = 25 mM). The increase of the mass recovery of positively-charged MP by B. subtilis is higher than E. coli when the IS was 5 mM; however, this phenomenon was reversed when the IS was 25 mM where E. coli increased the zeta potential of MP more negatively than that of B. subtilis, likely influencing MP mass recovery (He et al., 2021).
7. Discussion
Microplastic transport is affected by a multitude of physical, chemical, and biological parameters, many of which are intimately coupled. These include properties of: (i) the MPs—size, shape, concentration and type (density, surface roughness and wettability), and the MP zeta potential; (ii) the soil—texture, pore and particle sizes, mineralogy, the zeta potential of soil particle and soil organic matter; (iii) hydrological conditions—flow rate, wetting-drying cycles, saturation, and soil solution geochemistry including pH, ionic strength, and chemicals present in soil; and (iv) soil living organisms—plants, animals and microorganisms.
The complex dependence of MP mobility on various processes and properties can be illustrated by relating MP mobility to basic MP properties (type, size, and shape) and soil texture. When all basic parameters are plotted together no clear trend emerges, although some trends can be identified within subsets of the data, i.e. for specific properties (Fig. 4). Given these properties are categorical variables (i.e. with no inherent standalone numerical context), we categorized the data set into 17 unique subsets based on various MP characteristics (Table S7 in SI). However, due to limited amount of data in many of these series, we focus our analysis on a few subsets only. Considering flux-based mobility proxies as the dependent variable, we find for two subsets—(i) PE fragment in fine soil and (ii) PS beads in fine soil—a statistically significant negative correlation between MP size and flux-based mobility (p-values of 0.062 and 0.032, significant at the 10 % and 5 % levels, respectively). In contrast, for PS beads in very fine soil, MP size exhibit a statistically significant positive correlation with flux-based mobility (p-value = 0.046, significant at the 5 % level). For depth-based MP mobility, PE fragments in coarse soil exhibit a statistically significant negative correlation between MP size and depth (p-value = 0.018, significant at the 5 % level, see Figs. S3–5 in SI). Beyond these, no significant correlations were observed for other parameters, suggesting competing effects among these properties. Hence, the effect of some parameters on MP mobility depends on interactions with other parameters.
