A formidable source of complexity which challenges our understanding of MP mobility is the interaction of the aforementioned factors to affect mobility, termed here “co-effects”. By co-effects we refer here to the interference among factors, where the simultaneous changes in two or more parameters alter the competition between various factors and processes. These co-effects are behind the complex and often contradictory findings presented in this review, and the lack of clear trend in MP mobility when plotted vs. a single factor, e.g. the dependence of MP transport on MP size in Fig. 4. While the co-effect of MP sizes and soil pore size is intuitive—larger pore sizes allow larger MPs to pass through, other co-effects of MP size may be less clear. For instance, as MP size decreases below a certain threshold (which in turn could depend on multiple factors such as soil solution chemistry), additional mechanisms which affect transport become more dominant. For MP sizes in the range of 0.1–1 μm, a decrease in MP size decreases their Zeta potential, which enhances MP adsorption on the soil particles, reducing their mobility (reversing the trend for MPs larger than ∼1 μm). Aggregation of smaller plastic particles (< 0.1 μm, i.e. NPs) with ionic compounds from the soil solution (a process which by itself is also moderated by the plastics' zeta potential), increases the aggregates' hydrodynamic diameter; this, in turn, can reverse again the mobility trend to be inversely proportional (similar to the inverse proportionality between MP size and mobility in larger MPs, i.e. > 1 μm).
We note that only co-effects evidenced in the literature are presented in Fig. 5; we expect more co-effects to exist, for example, between the Zeta potentials of the MP and the soil, to be revealed upon further examination. Exploring the interactions among multiple factors and the resulting co-effects (Fig. 5) requires further experimental studies where all parameters besides those investigated are controlled and known. In many cases, multiple parameters that are not the control parameters are unknown or vary together with other parameters, challenging conclusive evidence on the specific effect of the actual control parameter of interest.
